{"event":"start","run":"R5-Paka-3","model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","effort":"medium","system":"You write one program in Paka. Reply with the whole file main.paka in exactly one\ncode block. Any other text is allowed; only the code block is used. A reply with no\ncode block or with several counts as a failed submission.\nThe harness builds it with: paka check main.paka\nIt runs the program on cases. Input comes as files named in the arguments.\nOutput is standard output, the exit status and, where the task says so, written files.\nThere is no standard input.\nAfter each submission you get the build result or the case results.\nYou have 8 submissions.\nUse only what Paka itself ships with: no outside packages.\nThe language reference follows.\n\n# Paka language reference\n\nAll of Paka is on this page, except visual apps: `spec/screens.md`. A block fenced as `paka` is a\nwhole program that `paka check` accepts. Any other block is a fragment, refused code or tool output.\n\n## 1. A program\n\n```paka\ntype Tally { words: Int = 0 }\n\nenum Msg { Start(path: String), Loaded(text: String), Failed(why: String) }\n\nfn count_words(text: String) -> Int {\n  len(words(text))\n}\n\nfn update(s: inout Tally, m: Msg) -> List<Effect<Msg>> {\n  match m {\n    Start(path) => [ReadFile(path, then: Loaded, or: Failed)]\n    Loaded(text) => {\n      s.words = count_words(text)\n      [Print(\"{s.words} words\"), Exit]\n    }\n    Failed(why) => [Print(\"cannot read file: {why}\"), ExitWith(20)]\n  }\n}\n\napp wc {\n  state: Tally\n  start: Start\n  update: update\n  grants: [ReadFiles, Console]\n}\n```\n\nA program is one `.paka` file, or every `.paka` file directly in one directory. It holds\ndefinitions in any order: `type`, `enum`, `fn`, `example` and at most one `app`. Definition,\nvariant and built-in names share one pool; each exists once (`duplicate-name`), but a definition\nor variant may take a built-in function's or element's name and replace it everywhere (`fn join`,\n`type Row`); a view then using that element is `duplicate-name`. An app may take any name.\n\nPaka has no user generics, interfaces, methods, lambdas, closures, tuples, null, exceptions,\nglobals, references or shared data. Functions cannot see anything but their parameters.\n\n## 2. Source text\n\n- UTF-8. Non-ASCII characters only inside strings and comments. No tab, no carriage return.\n- Names are ASCII letters, digits and `_`, and start with a letter. Upper first letter: types,\n  variants, effects, grants. Lower: functions, apps, examples, locals, parameters, fields, labels.\n- Keywords, never names: `app break continue else enum example false fn for foreign if in inout let\n  match migrate return true type var view while`.\n- `//` starts a comment that runs to the line end. No block comments.\n- A line break ends a statement. A line continues only inside an open `(` or `[`, after a line\n  ending in `,` `=` `->` `=>` `|` `{` or a two-sided operator, or before a line starting with\n  `}` `)` `]`. So: no `;` after a statement, `else` stands on the line of the `}` before it, and\n  `{ x }` may stand on one line. Fields and variants are separated by `,` or by a line break.\n- No trailing comma anywhere.\n- Nesting is at most 64 levels: each open `(` `[` `{`, type `<` and string `{`, and an `if` or\n  `match` in the condition or subject of another, and a `fn` type as the result of another. A\n  chain of operators, calls, fields or `?` has at most 1,000 links. Beyond either:\n  `nesting-too-deep`.\n\n## 3. Types\n\n| Type | Written | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Int` | `0` `42` `-7` | 64-bit signed |\n| `Float` | `1.5` `0.0` | IEEE 64-bit; digits on both sides of the point |\n| `Bool` | `true` `false` | |\n| `String` | `\"text\"` | a row of Unicode code points; one line |\n| `List<T>` | `[a, b]` `[]` | |\n| `Map<K, V>` | `[\"a\": 1]` `[:]` | K is `Int` or `String`; entries always in rising key order |\n| `Option<T>` | `Some(value)` `None` | |\n| `Result<T, E>` | `Ok(value)` `Err(error)` | E is any type |\n| `Effect<Msg>` | section 10 | |\n| `fn(A, B) -> R` | section 8 | a function type |\n\nNo Set (write `Map<K, Bool>`), no character type (a one-character `String`).\nNumber literals have no leading zero, hex, exponent or `_`.\n\n```\ntype Counter { count: Int = 0, name: String }   // record\nenum Shape { Dot, Circle(r: Float), Rect(w: Float, h: Float) }\nCounter { name: \"a\" }      // fields by name in declared order; one with a default may be left out\nc.count                    // field read\nCircle(1.5)                // variant fields by position; a variant without fields is written Dot\n```\n\n- Variants are written bare, never `Shape.Dot`, and a variant name is unique in the whole program.\n  An enum has at least one variant; variant fields have no defaults.\n- A default is a literal, `[]`, `[:]`, or a list or variant of defaults; anything else is\n  `unexpected-token`.\n- In the header of `if`, `while`, `for` and `match`, write a record in brackets: `(Counter {})`.\n- A type may hold itself only where a finite value exists: through `Option<T>`, a `List`, or an\n  enum with another variant (`enum Expr { Num(value: Int), Add(left: Expr, right: Expr) }`).\n  Otherwise `no-finite-value`.\n- Types are written on every parameter, result and field. A local takes the type of its value:\n  `let n = 1`. Values typed by their position (`[]`, `[:]`, `None`, `Ok(x)`, `Err(e)`, a hole,\n  `unreachable()`) need a parameter, field, result, operand or annotation to give the type, else\n  `cannot-infer`: write `let xs: List<Int> = []`. An `if` or `match` with no expected type takes\n  the type of its first branch that has one.\n\n## 4. Functions, statements, expressions\n\n```paka\ntype Stack { items: List<Int> = [] }\n\nenum Shape { Dot, Circle(r: Float), Rect(w: Float, h: Float) }\n\nfn clamp(x: Int; lo: Int, hi: Int) -> Int {\n  max(lo, min(x, hi))\n}\n\nfn area(s: Shape) -> Float {\n  match s {\n    Dot => 0.0\n    Circle(r) => r * r * 3.14159\n    Rect(w, h) => w * h\n  }\n}\n\nfn push(s: inout Stack, x: Int) {\n  append(&s.items, x)\n}\n\nfn sum_odd(n: Int) -> Int {\n  var total = 0\n  for i in 0..<n {\n    if i % 2 == 0 {\n      continue\n    }\n    total += i\n  }\n  total\n}\n\nfn sign(n: Int) -> String {\n  if n < 0 { \"minus\" } else if n == 0 { \"zero\" } else { \"plus\" }\n}\n\nexample tour {\n  var st = Stack {}\n  push(&st, clamp(12, lo: 0, hi: 9))\n  let r = Rect(2.0, 3.0)\n  st.items == [9] && area(r) == 6.0 && sum_odd(5) == 4 && sign(-1) == \"minus\"\n}\n```\n\n- Parameters before `;` are passed by position; those after it as `name: value`, in declared\n  order. A call writes exactly those labels. `f()` calls a function without parameters.\n- A function without `-> T` gives no value.\n- The value of a block is its last line. `return value` only leaves early, and a bare `return`\n  leaves a function without a result; on the last line it is the error `return-last`: drop\n  `return`.\n- `let x = e` is fixed; `var x = e` can be assigned. Either may carry a type: `let x: T = e`.\n- No shadowing: a `let`, `var`, parameter, `for` name or pattern name may not reuse a local name\n  visible there (`duplicate-local`). Blocks side by side may reuse a name. A local may hide a\n  function name.\n- Assignment: `place = e`, also `+= -= *= /= %=`. A place is a `var` local or an inout parameter,\n  then any `.field` and `[index]` steps: `g[r][c] = v`, `s.items[i].count += 1`.\n- `if c { } else if d { } else { }`. Used as a value it needs `else`.\n- `while c { }`; `for x in xs { }` walks a List or an Int range `a..<b` (b not included) and\n  nothing else: write `for c in chars(text)`, `for k in keys(m)`. `break`, `continue`.\n- `match`: one arm per line, no commas. A pattern is flat: `V`, `V(a, _)` (fields by position,\n  each a new name or `_`), an Int, a String, `true`, `false` or `_`. `A | B => ...` joins\n  patterns and binds no names. A match on an enum names every variant; a whole-pattern `_` is\n  allowed only when the subject is an Int, String or Bool. An arm is an expression, an assignment\n  or a block; `V => {}` does nothing.\n- A value must be used. A statement whose value is dropped is `unused-result`; write\n  `_ = pop(&q)`. A call that gives no value stands alone, without `_ =`.\n- An unused local is only a warning.\n- A statement after `return`, `break`, `continue` or `unreachable()` in the same block is the\n  error `unreachable-code`.\n\nOperators, tightest first: call, index, `.field`, `?`; unary `-` `!`; `*` `/` `%`; `+` `-`;\n`..<`; `==` `!=` `<` `<=` `>` `>=`; `&&`; `||`. Comparisons do not chain: write\n`a < b && b < c`. Evaluation is strictly left to right; `&&` and `||` skip the right side when the\nleft decides.\n\nBoth sides of an operator have the same type; nothing converts by itself (`to_float(n)`,\n`to_int(x)`).\n\n| Operator | Types |\n|---|---|\n| `==` `!=` | every type, compared by structure |\n| `<` `<=` `>` `>=` | Int, Float, String (by code point) |\n| `+` `+=` | Int, Float, String (joins) |\n| `-` `*` `/` | Int, Float |\n| `%` | Int |\n| `!` `&&` `\\|\\|` | Bool |\n\n## 5. Numbers and text\n\n- An Int result outside -9223372036854775808 to 9223372036854775807 traps `overflow`. `/`\n  truncates toward zero, `%` takes the sign of the left side, a zero divisor traps\n  `divide_by_zero`. Wrapping and bit operations are functions (section 9).\n- Float arithmetic never traps. NaN equals nothing, itself included.\n- String escapes, exactly these seven, no `\\u{hex}`: `\\n` `\\r` `\\t` `\\\\` `\\\"` `\\{` `\\}`. A\n  literal `{` is written `\\{`. A lone `}` and a `{` before the closing quote are text: `\"}\"`, `\"{\"`.\n- `\"{expr}\"` puts an Int, Bool or String into the text. Strings inside it hold no `{`:\n  `\"{join(xs, \" \")}\"`. A Float is written `\"{fixed(x, digits: 2)}\"`. No other type becomes text\n  by itself: build the text from its parts.\n- `s[i]` is a one-character String, `s[a..<b]` and `xs[a..<b]` are slices, each a new value.\n  Positions start at 0 and count code points; `len` counts the same. A position outside traps\n  `out_of_bounds`.\n- A String changes only as a whole: `s += \"x\"`. `s[i] = \"x\"` is refused.\n- A digit test: `c >= \"0\" && c <= \"9\"`.\n\n## 6. Values and `inout`\n\n- Every local, field and item is its own value. `let b = a` copies in meaning: changing one never\n  changes the other. Two places never share data.\n- Arguments are read-only. To let a function change the caller's data, declare the parameter\n  `inout` and mark the argument with `&`: `fn push(s: inout Stack, x: Int)`, `push(&st, 1)`.\n- `&` is written on every inout argument and nowhere else. After it comes a `var` local or an\n  inout parameter with any field and index steps (`&g.adj[a]`, `&m[key]`). Not a `let` (declare\n  it `var`), a read-only parameter (declare it `inout`), a `for` name or pattern name (they are\n  copies), a slice (pass the list and the positions) or a String position.\n- Two places overlap unless they start at different variables or first differ at two different\n  field names. Two index steps into one List or Map always overlap: `xs[i]` and `xs[j]`.\n- `inout-overlap`: a place passed with `&` overlaps another argument of the same call.\n  Fix: bind it first, `let old = xs` then `grow(&xs, old)`. For two items of one list, bind one\n  first or pass the list and both positions. Int, Float and Bool arguments are exempt; a String is not.\n- `changed-while-read`: one operand reads a place that a sibling operand changes. This holds for\n  operators, list, map and record literals and interpolation too. Fix: bind it first.\n- `changed-while-walked`: the body of `for x in xs` changes `xs` or anything overlapping it.\n  Fix: walk a copy (`let items = xs`) or walk by position (`for i in 0..<len(xs)`). `while`\n  freezes nothing.\n- The fields of a variant are not places. To change one, match, build the new value and assign\n  the subject: `Rect(w, h) => s = Rect(w + 1, h)`.\n- Trees are enums or records that hold themselves; using a subtree twice makes two copies.\n  Graphs use list positions: a node is its index. Build the adjacency lists with\n  `var adj: List<List<Int>> = list_of([], count: n)`; the annotation is needed (`cannot-infer`).\n\n```paka\ntype Graph { adj: List<List<Int>> = [] }\n\nfn dfs(g: Graph, seen: inout List<Bool>, u: Int) {\n  seen[u] = true\n  for v in g.adj[u] {\n    if !seen[v] { dfs(g, &seen, v) }\n  }\n}\n\nexample reach {\n  let g = Graph { adj: [[1], [0], []] }\n  var seen = list_of(false, count: 3)\n  dfs(g, &seen, 0)\n  seen == [true, true, false]\n}\n```\n\n## 7. Failure: `Option`, `Result`, `?`\n\n- A function that can fail or have no answer says so in its result: `Option<T>` or\n  `Result<T, E>`. Take them apart with `match`, `unwrap_or` or `?`.\n- Brackets claim, names ask. `xs[i]`, `m[key]` and `/` claim the position, key or divisor is\n  good, and trap when it is not. `get(m, key)`, `find`, `parse_int` and `parse_float` ask, and\n  give an `Option`.\n- `e?` gives the inside of `Some` or `Ok`; on `None` or `Err` the function returns that at once.\n  It may stand only in a function whose result is `Option<_>` (for an Option) or `Result<_, E>`\n  with the same `E` (for a Result). Not in `update`, not in an example.\n- An Option inside a Result function: `ok_or(opt, error)?`. A Result inside an Option function is\n  matched.\n- Changes made through an inout parameter before a `?` returns stay made.\n- An arm that returns, breaks or continues is a block: `None => {`, `return Err(\"empty\")`, `}` on three lines.\n- `unreachable()` stands where a value is expected but cannot be reached; it traps `assert`.\n- There is no `unwrap`, `expect`, `is_some`, `panic` or null.\n- A program reports a failure in `update`: `[Print(why), ExitWith(20)]`.\n\n```paka\nfn total(fields: List<String>) -> Result<Int, String> {\n  var sum = 0\n  for f in fields {\n    sum += ok_or(parse_int(f), \"not a number: {f}\")?\n  }\n  Ok(sum)\n}\n\nfn first_word(text: String) -> Option<String> {\n  let ws = words(text)\n  if len(ws) == 0 {\n    return None\n  }\n  Some(ws[0])\n}\n\nfn count_words(text: String) -> Map<String, Int> {\n  var counts: Map<String, Int> = [:]\n  for w in words(text) {\n    counts[w] = unwrap_or(get(counts, w), 0) + 1\n  }\n  counts\n}\n\nexample failures {\n  total([\"1\", \"2\"]) == Ok(3) && total([\"x\"]) == Err(\"not a number: x\") &&\n    first_word(\"\") == None && count_words(\"a b a\") == [\"a\": 2, \"b\": 1]\n}\n```\n\n## 8. Function values\n\n- A function value is the bare name of a user `fn` without inout parameters, or of a user variant\n  with fields (`Loaded` is a `fn(String) -> Msg`). Its type is written `fn(A, B) -> R`.\n- A call through a function value is positional and takes no `&`.\n- Built-in functions and built-in variants with fields are not values (`not-a-value`): wrap one in\n  a user `fn`. `None` and `Exit` are plain values.\n- There are no lambdas. Write a top-level function and pass its name:\n\n```\nfn by_score(a: Player, b: Player) -> Bool { a.score > b.score }\nsort_by(&players, by_score)\n```\n\n- Two function values are equal when they name the same definition.\n- An app's state may hold no function type.\n\n## 9. Built-in functions\n\n`T`, `K`, `V`, `E` stand for any type. All arguments are positional except the three labels\nshown (`digits:`, `at:`, `count:`); a `;` in a row only marks where the labels start, a call\nwrites `,`. A row without `->` gives no value. A row that changes its first argument takes it with `&`.\n\n```\nlen(x) -> Int                         String: code points; List: items; Map: entries\nis_empty(x) -> Bool                   len(x) == 0\ncontains(text, part) -> Bool          also contains(xs, item)\nfind(text, part) -> Option<Int>       first position; also find(xs, item)\n\nmin(a, b) -> T   max(a, b) -> T       Int, Float or String\nabs(x) -> T                           Int or Float\nto_float(n: Int) -> Float\nto_int(x: Float) -> Int               drops the fraction; traps bad_conversion on NaN or out of range\nsqrt(x) floor(x) ceil(x) trunc(x) round(x) -> Float    round: ties away from zero\nfixed(x: Float; digits: Int) -> String        digits 0 to 20; ties away from zero\nwrap_add(a, b) wrap_sub(a, b) wrap_mul(a, b) -> Int   wraps instead of trapping\nbit_and(a, b) bit_or(a, b) bit_xor(a, b) bit_not(a) -> Int\nshift_left(a, count) shift_right(a, count) -> Int      count 0 to 63\n\nparse_int(text) -> Option<Int>        \"-12\", \"007\"; nothing trimmed; None when not a number\nparse_float(text) -> Option<Float>\ncode_at(text, i) -> Int               the code point at position i\nfrom_code(n) -> String                one character\n\nsplit(text, sep) -> List<String>      split(\"a,,b\", \",\") is [\"a\", \"\", \"b\"]\nchars(text) -> List<String>           one String per character\nlines(text) -> List<String>           split on \"\\n\"; no empty last piece\nwords(text) -> List<String>           runs between white space, never empty\njoin(parts, sep) -> String\nrepeat(text, n) -> String\npad_left(text, width) -> String       spaces in front up to width\npad_right(text, width) -> String\ntrim(text) trim_start(text) trim_end(text) -> String     white space off the ends\nstarts_with(text, part) ends_with(text, part) -> Bool\nreplace(text, old, new) -> String     every match\nupper(text) lower(text) -> String     ASCII letters only\n\nappend(&xs, item)\nextend(&xs, more)                     appends every item of the List more\npop(&xs) -> T                         removes the last item; traps on an empty List\ninsert(&xs, item; at: Int)            at == len(xs) appends. Call: insert(&xs, x, at: 0)\nremove_at(&xs, i) -> T\nswap(&xs, i, j)\nreverse(&xs)\nsort(&xs)                             rising; List of Int, Float or String\nsort_by(&xs, before)                  before: fn(T, T) -> Bool\nlist_of(item; count: Int) -> List<T>  count copies\n\nget(m, key) -> Option<V>\nhas(m, key) -> Bool\nremove(&m, key)                       a missing key changes nothing\nkeys(m) -> List<K>                    in rising order\nvalues(m) -> List<V>                  in key order\n\nunwrap_or(opt, fallback) -> T         the inside of Some, else fallback\nok_or(opt, error) -> Result<T, E>     Some(x) gives Ok(x), None gives Err(error)\nassert(cond)                          traps assert when false\nunreachable()                         traps assert when reached\n```\n\n- White space is exactly six characters: code points 9 to 13 and 32.\n- Text is matched exactly, left to right, without overlap. No patterns.\n- The sort is stable: `before(a, b)` says whether `a` must come before `b`; ties keep their\n  order.\n- Map: `m[key]` reads and traps `out_of_bounds` when the key is missing. `m[key] = v` adds or\n  replaces. Other uses need the key present: `m[key] += 1` on a new key traps. The count idiom\n  is `counts[w] = unwrap_or(get(counts, w), 0) + 1`.\n- A bad position, a count below zero, `pop` on an empty List, a shift count or `digits` out of\n  range trap `out_of_bounds`.\n- There is no `map`, `filter`, `fold`, `sum`, `sorted` or `clamp`: loop.\n\n## 10. Apps and effects\n\n```\napp NAME {\n  state: S          // optional: a record with a default on every field, else `bad-state`\n  start: Start      // required: a variant of the message enum; its fields are all String\n  update: update    // required: the handler\n  grants: [Console] // required, may be []: Console, ReadFiles, WriteFiles\n}\n```\n\nOne key per line. The handler has exactly one of two shapes:\n\n```\nfn update(s: inout S, m: Msg) -> List<Effect<Msg>>     // with state:\nfn update(m: Msg) -> List<Effect<Msg>>                 // without state:\n```\n\n| Effect | Needs grant | Answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Print(text)` | `Console` | none; writes the text and a line feed to standard output |\n| `ReadFile(path; then: fn(String) -> Msg, or: fn(String) -> Msg)` | `ReadFiles` | `then(text)` or `or(reason)` |\n| `WriteFile(path, text; then: Msg, or: fn(String) -> Msg)` | `WriteFiles` | `then` or `or(reason)` |\n| `Exit` | none | ends with status 0 |\n| `ExitWith(status)` | none | ends with that status: 0 or 5 to 255 |\n\n`WriteFile`'s `then:` is a message value that may carry context:\n`WriteFile(path, text, then: Saved(path), or: Failed)`.\n\nEffects are plain values; only the list `update` returns is performed. `then:` and `or:`\nare both required. A reason is one of `not found`, `no permission`, `not a file`, `not text`,\n`no space`, `failed`. `WriteFile` replaces the whole file; `ReadFile` reads it all.\n\nHow a program runs:\n\n- The command-line arguments fill the fields of the `start:` variant by position. A wrong count\n  ends the run with status 2 and a usage line such as `wc: expected 1 argument (path), got 0`.\n  A first argument `--paka-examples` or `--paka-replay` is the tool's: unless served, status 2 too.\n- The state starts from its defaults; the start message is first in the queue.\n- The runtime takes the front message and calls `update`. When the handler has returned, its\n  effects are performed in list order. Each file answer joins the back of the queue.\n- Answers arrive in the order their effects were performed. Answers carry no path: keep in the\n  state what you wait for.\n- `Exit` and `ExitWith` end the program at once. Later effects are not performed, and a queued\n  answer is dropped.\n- A trap in a handler performs none of that handler's effects.\n- There is no standard input: data comes through arguments and files.\n\nFour stops end a program with one line on standard error and status 3:\n\n```\n<app>: effect <Effect> refused: grant <Grant> is missing\n<app>: effect Print failed: <reason>\n<app>: effect ExitWith failed: status <n> is not 0 or 5 to 255\n<app>: ended without Exit\n```\n\nA grant is tested as its effect is performed. Every path must reach `Exit` or\n`ExitWith`: an empty queue without one is the fourth stop. A literal status outside 0 or 5 to 255\nis the check error `literal-out-of-range`. A program without an app checks; `paka run` and\n`paka build` refuse it (`no-app`).\n\n## 11. Traps, holes, examples\n\nA trap ends the program with status 3. It cannot be caught. Evaluation order decides which trap\ncomes first. The eight kinds:\n\n| Kind | Raised by |\n|---|---|\n| `overflow` | Int arithmetic out of range |\n| `divide_by_zero` | Int `/` or `%` by 0 |\n| `out_of_bounds` | a bad index, slice, key, count or position |\n| `bad_conversion` | `to_int`, `from_code` |\n| `assert` | `assert(false)`, `unreachable()` |\n| `hole` | a hole reached |\n| `stack` | more than 10,000 nested calls |\n| `memory` | a List, Map or String beyond 16,777,216 items, entries or bytes |\n\n```\n<app>: trap <kind> at <file>:<line>:<col>\n  <detail>\n  in <function>, called from <function>\n```\n\nThe kind and the position are meaning; the two indented lines are help and may be absent.\n\nA hole `??name` stands for code not yet written; it takes the type its position expects.\n`paka check` accepts it (a note, status 0), reaching it traps `hole`, and `paka build` refuses it.\n\nAn example is `example name { ... }`: statements, then one Bool expression. It takes no\narguments, performs no effects and may not use `?`. Examples may call `update` on a `var` state\nand compare effect lists with `==`. They are checked with the program and run by\n`paka examples`. Each gets one status: `pass` (true), `fail` (false), `trapped` (a trap) or\n`unfinished` (a hole reached). Its line reads name, label, status; the label is `human-owned` when\nthe signed approval store holds it, else `agent-written`.\n\n## 12. Tools\n\n| Command | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `paka check <path> [--json]` | checks; prints the error, or warnings and hole notes |\n| `paka run <path> [arguments]` | checks, then runs the app |\n| `paka examples <path>` | checks, then runs every example |\n| `paka build <path> -o <output>` | checks, then writes an executable |\n| `paka gate <tree> --base <tree> [--json]` | refuses a merge whose approvals differ or owned examples fail; lists each |\n| `paka approve <path>` | the owner's: signs approved changes |\n\nExit status: 0 good; 1 a program error; 2 bad usage; 3 a trap, a stop or an example that\ndid not pass; 4 an internal error.\n\n`paka build` lists every hole, one line each; the others report only the first error, as one\nline. `paka check` adds one line per field:\n\n```\nmain.paka:4:3: error[type-mismatch]: expected `List<Effect<Msg>>`, found `String`\n  definition: update\n  bytes: 63..83\n  text: \"\\\"let there be light\\\"\"\n  expected: List<Effect<Msg>>\n  found: String\n```\n\nA `fix:` line follows when the error knows its repair. With `--json` the same error is one object\nwith the keys `kind`, `definition`, `message`, `file`, `start`, `end`, `line`, `column`, `text`,\n`expected`, `found`, `fix`. Lines and columns start at 1; columns count bytes.\n\n## 16. Foreign code\n\n```\nforeign Sum {\n  source: \"sum.c\"   // or library: \"z\"; beside the program\n  header: \"sum.h\"\n  Add(a: Int) -> Int = \"add\"\n}\n```\n\nEach line is an effect under grant `Sum`, answered like `ReadFile`; `then: Msg` with no result.\nArguments: Int, Float, Bool, String; result: Int, Float, Bool. The owner approves the grant,\ndeclaration and files. For now it answers `or(\"no host\")`.\n","at":1794833879290}
{"event":"call","n":1,"sent":"# Cheapest trips\n\nRead a file of roads between places and answer each trip question with the cheapest route.\n\n## Arguments\n\nThe program is run with exactly one argument: the name of the input file, which always exists and\ncan be read.\n\n## Input\n\nThe input file holds only the characters with codes 32 to 126 and the line feed (code 10). It is a\nsequence of lines: each line ends at a line feed, and the last line may lack one. An empty file has\nno lines. Lines are numbered from 1, blank lines included. The file has at most 200 lines, and no\nline is longer than 200 characters.\n\nA word is a run of characters other than the space, as long as possible. A name is 1 to 20\ncharacters, each a letter `A` to `Z` or `a` to `z`, a digit `0` to `9`, `_` or `-`. A line that\nholds no word is blank and is skipped. Every other line must hold exactly the words of one of:\n\n```\none FROM TO COST\ntwo FROM TO COST\ntrip FROM TO\n```\n\n`FROM` and `TO` are names and `COST` is 1 to 6 digits (`0` to `9`), leading zeros allowed: `007` is\n7 and `0` is 0.\n\n- `one FROM TO COST` is a road from `FROM` to `TO` that can be used only in that direction.\n- `two FROM TO COST` is two roads: one from `FROM` to `TO` and one from `TO` to `FROM`, each with\n  that cost.\n- `trip FROM TO` asks for the best route from `FROM` to `TO`.\n\nThe places are the names written on `one` and `two` lines. Several roads may join the same two\nplaces.\n\n## Routes\n\nA route from place `A` to place `B` is a sequence of roads in which the first starts at `A`, each\nnext one starts where the one before it ends, and the last ends at `B`. It may pass a place more\nthan once. Its cost is the sum of the costs of its roads. From a place to itself there is also the\nroute with no roads, of cost 0. A route is written as the places it visits, in order, `A` first and\n`B` last: one more place than it has roads.\n\nThe best route is the one with the lowest cost. When several routes have that cost, the best is the\none of them with the fewest roads. When several of those remain, compare their places one by one\nfrom the first: the best route is the one whose place comes first in ASCII order at the first place\nwhere they differ. A name that is the start of a longer name comes first.\n\nEach trip is answered with every road of the file, also the roads on lines after it.\n\n## Output\n\nPrint one line per trip, in file order. The line starts with `FROM to TO: ` and goes on with the\nfirst of these that applies:\n\n1. `unknown FROM` when `FROM` is not a place;\n2. `unknown TO` when `TO` is not a place;\n3. `no route` when there is no route;\n4. `cost C, R roads: P1 P2 ...` with `C` the cost and `R` the number of roads of the best route, in\n   decimal, then its places separated by single spaces. When `R` is 1 the word is `road`.\n\nEvery printed line ends with a line feed. The exit status is 0.\n\nExample input:\n\n```\ntwo home mill 4\none home bank 1\none bank mill 3\ntwo mill park 2\none home yard 5\ntrip home park\ntrip park bank\ntrip park park\ntrip home zoo\ntwo yard park 1\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nhome to park: cost 6, 2 roads: home mill park\npark to bank: cost 7, 3 roads: park mill home bank\npark to park: cost 0, 0 roads: park\nhome to zoo: unknown zoo\n```\n\nFrom `home` to `park` three routes cost 6: `home mill park` and `home yard park` with 2 roads, and\n`home bank mill park` with 3. Of the first two, `mill` comes before `yard`. The road from `yard` to\n`park` is on the last line and still counts.\n\n## Errors\n\nEach error prints exactly one line, ending with a line feed, and nothing else, and exits with\nstatus 20. `N` below is a line number in decimal.\n\nThe lines are checked in order from line 1, and only the first line with an error is reported:\n\n- The line is not blank and does not hold exactly the words of one of the three forms above:\n  `error: line N: bad line`\n- It is a `one` or `two` line whose `FROM` and `TO` are the same name: `error: line N: loop road`\n\nWhen every line is good but the file has no trip line, the error is `error: no trips`.\n\n## Visible cases\n\n### Case 1\n\nArguments: \"roads.txt\"\n\nFile `roads.txt`:\n```\ntwo home mill 4\none home bank 1\none bank mill 3\ntwo mill park 2\none home yard 5\ntrip home park\ntrip park bank\ntrip park park\ntrip home zoo\ntwo yard park 1\n```\n\nExpected standard output:\n```\nhome to park: cost 6, 2 roads: home mill park\npark to bank: cost 7, 3 roads: park mill home bank\npark to park: cost 0, 0 roads: park\nhome to zoo: unknown zoo\n```\n\nExpected exit status: 0\n\n### Case 2\n\nArguments: \"long.txt\"\n\nFile `long.txt`:\n```\ntrip a b\ntwo a b 1234567\n```\n\nExpected standard output:\n```\nerror: line 2: bad line\n```\n\nExpected exit status: 20\n","at":1794833879295}
{"event":"reply","n":1,"text":"Not run through `paka check`, so it may not compile on the first try. The program reads the file, validates every line in order, then builds the road graph. For each trip it runs Dijkstra backward from the destination, packing cost and road count into one key (`cost*100000 + roads`). It then walks forward from the start, always taking the alphabetically smallest next place that stays on a best route.\n\n```paka\ntype Road { a: Int, b: Int, w: Int }\n\ntype Edge { to: Int, w: Int }\n\nenum Msg { Start(path: String), Loaded(text: String), Failed(why: String) }\n\nfn is_name(s: String) -> Bool {\n  let n = len(s)\n  if n < 1 || n > 20 {\n    return false\n  }\n  for c in chars(s) {\n    let ok = (c >= \"a\" && c <= \"z\") || (c >= \"A\" && c <= \"Z\") || (c >= \"0\" && c <= \"9\") || c == \"_\" || c == \"-\"\n    if !ok {\n      return false\n    }\n  }\n  true\n}\n\nfn is_cost(s: String) -> Bool {\n  let n = len(s)\n  if n < 1 || n > 6 {\n    return false\n  }\n  for c in chars(s) {\n    if !(c >= \"0\" && c <= \"9\") {\n      return false\n    }\n  }\n  true\n}\n\nfn place(ids: inout Map<String, Int>, names: inout List<String>, s: String) -> Int {\n  match get(ids, s) {\n    Some(x) => x\n    None => {\n      let k = len(names)\n      ids[s] = k\n      append(&names, s)\n      k\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfn shortest(radj: List<List<Edge>>, n: Int, t: Int) -> List<Int> {\n  let inf = 1000000000000000\n  var dist = list_of(inf, count: n)\n  var done = list_of(false, count: n)\n  dist[t] = 0\n  while true {\n    var best = -1\n    for i in 0..<n {\n      if !done[i] && dist[i] < inf {\n        if best < 0 || dist[i] < dist[best] {\n          best = i\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    if best < 0 {\n      break\n    }\n    done[best] = true\n    for e in radj[best] {\n      let nd = dist[best] + e.w * 100000 + 1\n      if nd < dist[e.to] {\n        dist[e.to] = nd\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  dist\n}\n\nfn solve(text: String) -> Result<List<String>, String> {\n  let ls = lines(text)\n  var trips = 0\n  for i in 0..<len(ls) {\n    let ws = words(ls[i])\n    let num = i + 1\n    if len(ws) == 0 {\n      continue\n    }\n    let kind = ws[0]\n    if kind == \"trip\" {\n      if len(ws) != 3 || !is_name(ws[1]) || !is_name(ws[2]) {\n        return Err(\"error: line {num}: bad line\")\n      }\n      trips += 1\n    } else if kind == \"one\" || kind == \"two\" {\n      if len(ws) != 4 || !is_name(ws[1]) || !is_name(ws[2]) || !is_cost(ws[3]) {\n        return Err(\"error: line {num}: bad line\")\n      }\n      if ws[1] == ws[2] {\n        return Err(\"error: line {num}: loop road\")\n      }\n    } else {\n      return Err(\"error: line {num}: bad line\")\n    }\n  }\n  if trips == 0 {\n    return Err(\"error: no trips\")\n  }\n  var ids: Map<String, Int> = [:]\n  var names: List<String> = []\n  var roads: List<Road> = []\n  for i in 0..<len(ls) {\n    let ws = words(ls[i])\n    if len(ws) == 0 {\n      continue\n    }\n    if ws[0] != \"trip\" {\n      let a = place(&ids, &names, ws[1])\n      let b = place(&ids, &names, ws[2])\n      let w = unwrap_or(parse_int(ws[3]), 0)\n      append(&roads, Road { a: a, b: b, w: w })\n      if ws[0] == \"two\" {\n        append(&roads, Road { a: b, b: a, w: w })\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  let n = len(names)\n  var adj: List<List<Edge>> = list_of([], count: n)\n  var radj: List<List<Edge>> = list_of([], count: n)\n  for r in roads {\n    append(&adj[r.a], Edge { to: r.b, w: r.w })\n    append(&radj[r.b], Edge { to: r.a, w: r.w })\n  }\n  let inf = 1000000000000000\n  var out: List<String> = []\n  for i in 0..<len(ls) {\n    let ws = words(ls[i])\n    if len(ws) == 0 {\n      continue\n    }\n    if ws[0] == \"trip\" {\n      let f = ws[1]\n      let t = ws[2]\n      let prefix = \"{f} to {t}: \"\n      if !has(ids, f) {\n        append(&out, prefix + \"unknown \" + f)\n      } else if !has(ids, t) {\n        append(&out, prefix + \"unknown \" + t)\n      } else {\n        let s = ids[f]\n        let d = ids[t]\n        let dist = shortest(radj, n, d)\n        if dist[s] >= inf {\n          append(&out, prefix + \"no route\")\n        } else {\n          var parts: List<String> = [f]\n          var u = s\n          while u != d {\n            var best = -1\n            for e in adj[u] {\n              if dist[e.to] < inf && dist[e.to] + e.w * 100000 + 1 == dist[u] {\n                if best < 0 || names[e.to] < names[best] {\n                  best = e.to\n                }\n              }\n            }\n            append(&parts, names[best])\n            u = best\n          }\n          let cost = dist[s] / 100000\n          let hops = dist[s] % 100000\n          var word = \"roads\"\n          if hops == 1 {\n            word = \"road\"\n          }\n          append(&out, prefix + \"cost {cost}, {hops} {word}: \" + join(parts, \" \"))\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  Ok(out)\n}\n\nfn update(m: Msg) -> List<Effect<Msg>> {\n  match m {\n    Start(path) => [ReadFile(path, then: Loaded, or: Failed)]\n    Loaded(text) => {\n      match solve(text) {\n        Ok(ls) => [Print(join(ls, \"\\n\")), Exit]\n        Err(e) => [Print(e), ExitWith(20)]\n      }\n    }\n    Failed(why) => [Print(\"cannot read file: {why}\"), ExitWith(20)]\n  }\n}\n\napp trips {\n  start: Start\n  update: update\n  grants: [ReadFiles, Console]\n}\n```","input":12181,"output":5487,"cut_off":false,"ms":37335,"at":1794833916633}
